<edge source="global_energy_supply_ris::crude_flow_rate" target="ai_compute_chip_race::spot_price_level" id="0">
  <data key="d28">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d34">-</data>
  <data key="d29">3</data>
  <data key="d36">0.9</data>
  <data key="d37">An increase in aggregate crude flows raises near-term supply available to markets and physical delivery, directly reducing immediate scarcity and pushing spot prices down (and vice versa when flows drop).</data>
  <data key="d38">0-6 months</data>
  <data key="d39">global_energy_supply_ris</data>
  <data key="d40">ai_compute_chip_race</data>
</edge>
<edge source="global_energy_supply_ris::crude_flow_rate" target="china_eu_trade_rivalry::trade_flow_volume" id="0">
  <data key="d28">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d34">+</data>
  <data key="d29">3</data>
  <data key="d36">0.9</data>
  <data key="d37">Higher aggregate crude flow directly raises the quantity of energy cargoes moving through international routes, increasing measured trade flow volumes between producers and major consumers (including China and the EU).</data>
  <data key="d38">0-6 months</data>
  <data key="d39">global_energy_supply_ris</data>
  <data key="d40">china_eu_trade_rivalry</data>
</edge>
<edge source="global_energy_supply_ris::crude_flow_rate" target="china_eu_trade_rivalry::tanker_attack_risk" id="0">
  <data key="d28">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d34">+</data>
  <data key="d29">1</data>
  <data key="d36">0.55</data>
  <data key="d37">Increased crude tanker traffic raises the density of valuable, soft targets and transit interactions in chokepoints, modestly raising the probability of hostile incidents against shipping.</data>
  <data key="d38">0-6 months</data>
  <data key="d39">global_energy_supply_ris</data>
  <data key="d40">china_eu_trade_rivalry</data>
</edge>
<edge source="global_energy_supply_ris::crude_flow_rate" target="iran_proxy_escalation::security_incident_freq" id="0">
  <data key="d28">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d34">+</data>
  <data key="d29">3</data>
  <data key="d36">0.7</data>
  <data key="d37">A sustained drop in global crude flow (especially through MENA choke points) increases Tehran's incentive to use proxy attacks on shipping and energy infrastructure to raise costs, signal deterrence and extract political leverage, driving up the frequency of security incidents.</data>
  <data key="d38">0-6 months</data>
  <data key="d39">global_energy_supply_ris</data>
  <data key="d40">iran_proxy_escalation</data>
</edge>
<edge source="global_energy_supply_ris::crude_flow_rate" target="russia_ukraine_war::market_price_volatility" id="0">
  <data key="d28">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d34">-</data>
  <data key="d29">2</data>
  <data key="d36">0.7</data>
  <data key="d37">An increase in the aggregate crude flow rate eases tightness in physical markets and reduces the sensitivity of prices to discrete disruptions, thereby damping short- to medium-term wholesale price swings.</data>
  <data key="d38">0-6 months</data>
  <data key="d39">global_energy_supply_ris</data>
  <data key="d40">russia_ukraine_war</data>
</edge>
